          "letter": "B",
          "english": "Glimmers(少许) of hope, however, are beginning to emerge in this bruising environment: Americans are becoming aware of the toll their jobs take on them, and employers are exploring ways to alleviate the harmful effects of stress and overwork. Yet much more work remains to be done. To call stress an epidemic isn't an exaggeration. The 83% of American employees who are stressed about their jobs—up from 73% just a year before—say that poor compensation and an unreasonable workload are their number-one sources of stress. And if you suspected that the workplace had gotten more stressful than it was just a few decades ago, you're right. Stress levels increased 18% for women and 24% for men from 1983 to 2009. Stress is also starting earlier in life, with some data suggesting that today's teens are even more stressed than adults.",
          "chinese": "然而，在这个令人疲惫不堪的环境中，希望的微光正开始显现：美国人逐渐意识到工作给他们带来的损耗，而雇主也在探索减轻压力和过度工作的有害影响的方法。但仍有大量工作需要完成。把压力称为一种流行病并不夸张。那些因工作感到压力的美国员工比例已从仅仅一年前的73%上升到83%，他们表示，报酬低和工作量不合理是压力的首要来源。如果你怀疑职场比几十年前压力更大，那么你的判断是正确的。从1983年到2009年，女性的压力水平上升了18%，男性上升了24%。压力也开始在人生更早阶段出现，一些数据显示，如今的青少年甚至比成年人承受着更大的压力。",
